import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var o={moonPhases:{theory:{concept:"[[The phases of the Moon are the changing shapes of the lit part of the Moon that we see from the Earth.]] The Moon does not shine by itself: it reflects sunlight, and the Sun always lights exactly one half of it. A full cycle of phases takes about 29.5 days."},section:{halfLit:{title:"Half is always lit",text:"Look at the Moon in the top view: whatever the phase, the half that faces the Sun is bright and the other half is dark \u2014 just like the Earth has day and night. The Moon's shape does not change and nothing covers it. Only the direction from which we look at it changes."},whatWeSee:{title:"Which half do we see?",text:"[[From the Earth we can see only the half of the Moon that faces us (on the Earth's side of the dashed line).]] The phase is the part of that half which is also lit. When the Moon is between the Earth and the Sun, its lit half faces away: new Moon. When the Earth is between the Moon and the Sun, we see the whole lit half: full Moon. In between, we see part of each: a crescent, a half (a quarter) or a gibbous Moon."},cycle:{title:"Waxing and waning",text:"After the new Moon the lit part grows \u2014 the Moon is waxing: crescent, first quarter, gibbous, full. Then it shrinks \u2014 it is waning: gibbous, last quarter, crescent, and new again. The lit fraction depends only on the angle E between the Moon and the Sun seen from the Earth: k = (1 \u2212 cos E)/2. At E = 90\xB0 exactly half of the disc is lit."},sky:{title:"When can we see it?",text:"The Moon's phase also tells you when it is up. A full Moon is opposite the Sun: it rises at sunset and is highest at midnight. The first-quarter Moon is highest at about 6 pm, so it is seen in the evening; the last-quarter Moon is highest at about 6 am and is seen in the morning. The new Moon is up during the day, next to the Sun, and is lost in its glare. In the southern hemisphere people see the Moon upside down, so there the waxing Moon is lit on the left."},month:{title:"Two different months",text:"The Moon goes once round the Earth, measured against the stars, in P = 27.32 days (the sidereal month). But the Earth also moves round the Sun, about 1\xB0 a day, so the Moon needs about two more days to catch up with the Sun again. That is why from new Moon to new Moon takes S = 29.53 days (the synodic month): 1/S = 1/P \u2212 1/Y. Turn off the Earth's motion and the two months become equal."},eclipses:{title:"Why not an eclipse every month?",text:"[[At new Moon the Moon could hide the Sun (a solar eclipse), and at full Moon it could enter the Earth's shadow (a lunar eclipse).]] But the Moon's orbit is tilted by about 5\xB0 to the Earth's orbit, so usually the Moon passes a little above or below the Sun or the shadow. An eclipse happens only when a new or full Moon falls near one of the two nodes \u2014 the points where the Moon crosses the plane of the Earth's orbit. That happens about twice a year, in 'eclipse seasons'. Turn the 3D picture and look along the plane of the Earth's orbit: at most new and full Moons the Moon is above or below the dark shadow cones; only when it is near the dashed line of the nodes does it pass through them."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"Is the Moon's dark part the Earth's shadow? (No \u2014 the Earth's shadow falls on the Moon only during a lunar eclipse; the dark part is simply the Moon's own night side.) What phase does someone on the Moon see the Earth in when we see a full Moon? (A 'new Earth': the opposite phase.) Can you see a full Moon at noon? (No \u2014 it is below the horizon.) Test each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{illuminated:"Fraction of the Moon's disc that is lit (E: angle between the Moon and the Sun)",age:"Age of the Moon: days since the last new Moon",transit:"Time when the Moon is highest in the sky (local solar time)",synodic:"Synodic month from the sidereal month and the year",latitude:"Height of the Moon above the plane of the Earth's orbit"},variable:{elongation:"Angle between the Moon and the Sun, seen from the Earth",meanElongation:"How far the Moon has moved east of the Sun (0\u2013360\xB0)",illuminated:"Lit fraction of the disc",age:"Age of the Moon",synodic:"Synodic month (new Moon to new Moon)",sidereal:"Sidereal month (one turn against the stars)",year:"Year (one turn of the Earth round the Sun)",inclination:"Tilt of the Moon's orbit",latitude:"Moon's height above the Earth's orbit (seen from the Earth)"},assumption:{circles:"Both orbits are circles travelled at constant speed (the real Moon's distance changes by about 5 %).",parallel:"Sunlight reaches the Moon and the Earth as parallel rays: the Sun is about 390 times farther away than the Moon.",nodes:"The Moon's orbit keeps its direction in space (in reality its nodes turn once in 18.6 years).",times:"Rise and highest-point times are for an observer on the equator and ignore the Moon's tilt and its motion during a day; real times can differ by an hour or more.",air:"The Earth's air, which makes the Moon look red during a lunar eclipse and widens the shadow slightly, is ignored.",scale:"Not to scale: the real Moon is 30 Earth diameters away, and the Sun far beyond the picture. The tilt of the Moon's orbit can be drawn five times larger so that it is easy to see, and the shadows are drawn as narrowing cones whose size is not to scale."},param:{startAge:"Phase at the start (days after new Moon)",daysPerSecond:"Speed of time",hemisphere:"Observer",earthOrbits:"The Earth goes round the Sun",siderealMonth:"One turn against the stars (sidereal month)",timeStep:"Time step",inclination:"Tilt of the Moon's orbit",nodeLongitude:"Direction of the nodes (from the Sun at the start)"},hint:{startAge:"0 is a new Moon, about 15 days is a full Moon. The run starts from this phase.",daysPerSecond:"How many days pass in one second of the animation.",hemisphere:"In the southern hemisphere the Moon looks upside down.",earthOrbits:"Turn this off to see how long a month would be if the Sun stayed in the same direction.",siderealMonth:"The real Moon needs 27.32 days. A closer moon would go round faster.",inclination:"The angle between the Moon's orbit and the Earth's orbit. 0\xB0 means the two orbits lie in one plane.",nodeLongitude:"The nodes are where the Moon crosses the plane of the Earth's orbit. 0\xB0 points them at the Sun at the start."},option:{north:"Northern hemisphere",south:"Southern hemisphere"},preset:{fast:"Fast",moon:"Moon",slow:"Slow",flat:"Flat",towardsSun:"Towards the Sun",sideways:"Sideways"},phase:{newMoon:"New Moon",waxingCrescent:"Waxing crescent",firstQuarter:"First quarter",waxingGibbous:"Waxing gibbous",fullMoon:"Full Moon",waningGibbous:"Waning gibbous",lastQuarter:"Last quarter",waningCrescent:"Waning crescent"},measure:{illuminated:"Lit part of the disc",age:"Age of the Moon",time:"Time since the start",elongation:"Angle between the Moon and the Sun",transitTime:"Highest in the sky at (hour)",riseTime:"Rises at (hour)",synodicMonth:"From new Moon to new Moon",siderealMonth:"One turn against the stars",phaseAngle:"Phase angle (Sun \u2013 Moon \u2013 Earth)",sunLongitude:"Sun's direction has turned by",months:"Months since the start",latitude:"Moon's height above the Earth's orbit"},view:{sunlight:"Sunlight",earth:"Earth",moon:"Moon",star:"A distant star",time:"Day {day} \xB7 age of the Moon {age}",legend:"Dashed ring: the half of the Moon facing the Earth \xB7 Moon's orbit: solid above the plane of the Earth's orbit, dashed below (tilt \xD7{factor}) \xB7 dark cones: shadows",seenNorth:"Seen from the Northern hemisphere",seenSouth:"Seen from the Southern hemisphere",phase:"{phase} \xB7 {lit} lit",highest:"Highest in the sky at about {time}",solarEclipse:"Solar eclipse",lunarEclipse:"Lunar eclipse",positionsOption:"Where each phase happens",facingOption:"The half facing the Earth",motionOption:"Direction of motion",starsOption:"Direction to a distant star",nodes:"nodes: eclipses only here",shadowsOption:"Shadows of the Earth and the Moon",planeOption:"Plane of the Earth's orbit and the nodes",tiltOption:"Tilt of the Moon's orbit \xD75 (exaggerated)"},graph:{illuminated:"Lit part of the Moon over time",elongation:"Angle between the Moon and the Sun",latitude:"Moon above and below the Earth's orbit"},challenge:{fullMoon:{title:"Full Moon",description:"Show a full Moon: the whole disc lit.",hint:"The Earth must be between the Sun and the Moon. Try the 'Full Moon' preset."},halfMoon:{title:"Half a Moon",description:"Make exactly half of the Moon's disc lit.",hint:"The angle between the Moon and the Sun must be 90\xB0: a quarter of the month after a new or full Moon."},morningCrescent:{title:"The crescent before sunrise",description:"Show the thin old crescent that is seen in the east just before dawn.",hint:"It comes a few days before the new Moon: start at about 25\u201327 days."},southQuarter:{title:"The first quarter in Australia",description:"Show the first-quarter Moon as it is seen from the southern hemisphere. Which side is lit?",hint:"Choose the southern observer and the 'First quarter' preset."},equalMonths:{title:"Two months become one",description:"Make the month from new Moon to new Moon as long as one turn against the stars.",hint:"The difference comes from the Earth's motion round the Sun."},twentyDays:{title:"A month of 20 days",description:"With the Earth going round the Sun, make a moon whose phases repeat every 20 days (\xB10.1 d).",hint:"1/S = 1/P \u2212 1/Y: the sidereal month must be a little shorter than 20 days."},solarEclipse:{title:"A solar eclipse",description:"Keep the real tilt of the Moon's orbit (at least 5\xB0) and make a solar eclipse happen.",hint:"The new Moon must be near a node: point the nodes at the Sun and start at a new Moon \u2014 or wait about three months."},lunarEclipse:{title:"A lunar eclipse",description:"Keep the real tilt of the Moon's orbit (at least 5\xB0) and put the Moon into the Earth's shadow.",hint:"The full Moon must be near a node: point the nodes at the Sun and start at a full Moon."}}}},n={sim:o};export{n as default,o as sim};
